`identifiers` is an `array<struct>` with exactly 6 entries built statically (no loop, no conditional `when`, no filter step). Each entry is a struct with 2 outer fields: `key` (a string literal label) and `value` (a nested struct with 4 fields). The inner `value` struct schema is uniform across all 6 entries: (i) `reference_id` — `lit(None).cast('string')` (always NULL placeholder); (ii) `identifier_type` — a string literal that equals the outer `key`; (iii) `identifier` — the actual identifier value; (iv) `identifier_source` — `lit('meridian')` (always the literal string 'meridian'). The 6 entries are:
1. key='claim_id', identifier=`col('claim_id_hdr')` (direct column);
2. key='subscriber_id', identifier=`col('subscriber_id_hdr')` (direct column);
3. key='member_id', identifier=`col('member_id_hdr')` (direct column);
4. key='payer_claim_control_number', identifier=`col('icn_hdr')` (direct column, ICN = internal control number);
5. key='provider_claim_id', identifier=`concat_ws('_', col('claim_id_hdr'), col('billing_npi_prov'))` — THE ONLY DERIVED ENTRY: joins the claim_id and the billing-provider NPI with an underscore separator;
6. key='group_number', identifier=`col('group_number_hdr')` (direct column).
Entries 1–4 and 6 are pure column-passthrough; entry 5 ('provider_claim_id') is the only computed identifier in the array.

